<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Garigliano</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Garigliano river</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Garigliano is a river in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, formed by the Liri and Gari rivers and flowing into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-gaeta">Gulf of Gaeta</a>. In the late 9th century Arab raiders built a fortified base near its mouth, destroyed in 915 by a Christian coalition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and southern Italian princes. In 1503 the Spanish under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-fernandez-de-cordoba">Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</a> defeated the French on its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/garigliano.jpg" alt="Garigliano" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MM · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GariglianoFoce.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
